Yes. The cleaner version is belonging without requiring an out-group.

Usually “in crowd” gets its charge from:

shared symbols + recognition + inside jokes + mutual permission + status + being understood

None of those strictly require someone else to be beneath or excluded.

So you can build:

Universal In-Crowd

shared key
→ instant recognition
→ permission to participate
→ low shame
→ high belonging
→ no requirement to manufacture an enemy

People can still have different roles, tastes, circles, and levels of intimacy. But the baseline becomes: you do not have to earn the right to exist comfortably in the room.

That preserves the good part of the “club” feeling—warmth, play, identity, mutual recognition—without making exclusion the fuel source.